 \maketitle
 
 \begin{abstract}
-\noindent We describe how GDP per inhabitant in purchasing power standards (PPS) varies across 236 EU27 NUTS-2 regions in 2024 and how strongly high- and low-income regions cluster in space. Regional income spans a factor of more than six, from 16,700 PPS in the poorest to 107,200 PPS in the richest region. Neighbouring regions resemble each other: global Moran's $I$ is 0.37 under queen contiguity and remains clearly positive under three alternative weights definitions (five and eight nearest neighbours, inverse distance). The clustering is not an artefact of a few metropolitan peaks; dropping the top five regions raises it. It is, however, almost entirely a between-country phenomenon: once each region's income is measured relative to its national mean, spatial autocorrelation disappears. Local indicators locate low-income clusters in Poland, Greece, Bulgaria, Romania and Hungary and high-income clusters in Germany, the Netherlands, Ireland and northern Italy; after a false discovery rate correction, 22 local clusters remain (20 low-low, one high-high, one high-low). We report associations only and make no causal claim.
+\noindent We describe how GDP per inhabitant in purchasing power standards (PPS) varies across 236 EU27 NUTS-2 regions in 2024 and how strongly high- and low-income regions cluster in space. Regional income spans a factor of more than six, from 16,700 PPS in the poorest to 107,200 PPS in the richest region. Neighbouring regions resemble each other: global Moran's $I$ is 0.37 under queen contiguity and remains clearly positive under three alternative weights definitions (five and eight nearest neighbours, inverse distance). The clustering is not an artefact of a few metropolitan peaks; dropping the top five regions raises it. It is, however, largely a between-country phenomenon: once each region's income is measured relative to its national mean, Moran's $I$ falls to 0.02, although this test has low power and its permutation null is not adapted to demeaned data. Uncorrected local indicators suggest low-income clusters in Poland, Greece, Bulgaria, Romania and Hungary and high-income clusters in Germany, the Netherlands, Ireland and northern Italy; after a false discovery rate correction, only 22 regions remain significant (20 low-low, one high-high, one high-low), so the high-income clusters are suggestive only. We report associations only and make no causal claim.
 
 \medskip
 \noindent\textbf{Keywords:} regional inequality; GDP per capita; purchasing power standards; NUTS 2; spatial autocorrelation; Moran's I; LISA; European Union
